1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,099
Priya Malhotra manages renewals for forty one commercial insurance clients.

2
00:00:05,099 --> 00:00:10,039
On a normal week her inbox fills with the same shape of noise every morning: a

3
00:00:10,039 --> 00:00:14,155
webinar invite, a LinkedIn digest, a flash sale on office chairs.

4
00:00:14,155 --> 00:00:19,094
Somewhere in the middle of that Tuesday's forty emails sat a message from Ravi

5
00:00:19,094 --> 00:00:24,350
Chandra at Brightwell Manufacturing, asking a simple question about their September

6
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:24,857
renewal.

7
00:00:24,857 --> 00:00:26,313
Nobody had answered it.

8
00:00:26,313 --> 00:00:27,833
Nobody had even seen it.

9
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:29,416
That is not carelessness.

10
00:00:29,416 --> 00:00:34,546
That is what forty identical looking subject lines do to a human inbox by Tuesday

11
00:00:34,546 --> 00:00:35,179
lunchtime.

12
00:00:35,179 --> 00:00:37,395
And the stakes are not symmetrical.

13
00:00:37,395 --> 00:00:40,688
If Priya misses the webinar invite, nothing happens.

14
00:00:40,688 --> 00:00:45,818
If she misses Ravi's question long enough, a commercial policy drifts towards its

15
00:00:45,818 --> 00:00:51,010
renewal date unanswered, and a lapsed policy is not a late invoice: it is a client

16
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:52,150
sitting uninsured.

17
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:57,343
Six days of silence had already been absorbed before anyone knew there was silence

18
00:00:57,343 --> 00:00:57,913
to count.

19
00:00:57,913 --> 00:00:59,749
Every inbox has one of these.

20
00:00:59,749 --> 00:01:03,549
The whole point of the next twenty minutes is to find yours.

21
00:01:04,099 --> 00:01:07,118
You are not building anything from scratch yet.

22
00:01:07,118 --> 00:01:10,265
Section 3 does that, slowly and screen by screen.

23
00:01:10,265 --> 00:01:15,405
Right now you are doing what most people do with a new agent for the first time:

24
00:01:15,405 --> 00:01:19,837
importing someone else's work and seeing whether it earns your trust.

25
00:01:19,837 --> 00:01:23,884
Open the course resources and grab the file called C01-preview.

26
00:01:23,884 --> 00:01:29,151
It is a real n8n workflow, saved out as one plain text file that n8n can read back

27
00:01:29,151 --> 00:01:32,813
in, and it is the same file I used to write this lecture.

28
00:01:32,813 --> 00:01:35,061
A word on how to take this lecture.

29
00:01:35,061 --> 00:01:40,393
If you already have n8n and a free AI account connected from skimming ahead, follow

30
00:01:40,393 --> 00:01:41,100
along live.

31
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:46,431
If not, watch it through once first, then come back and do it with the video paused

32
00:01:46,431 --> 00:01:47,266
at each step.

33
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:48,358
Both routes work.

34
00:01:48,358 --> 00:01:53,562
The only wrong route is watching without ever running it, because the moment this

35
00:01:53,562 --> 00:01:58,508
course actually starts is the moment the agent reads your inbox, not Priya's.

36
00:01:59,058 --> 00:02:04,090
The first thing n8n shows a new account is not a blank terminal, it is one button.

37
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:08,753
You will use the menu next to it to import the file instead of starting from

38
00:02:08,753 --> 00:02:13,539
scratch, and within a few seconds the canvas fills in with three boxes already

39
00:02:13,539 --> 00:02:17,405
connected: a trigger, an agent, and the model behind the agent.

40
00:02:17,955 --> 00:02:21,612
That is the whole shape of everything you build in this course.

41
00:02:21,612 --> 00:02:25,676
Something happens, an agent looks at it, the agent decides what to do.

42
00:02:25,676 --> 00:02:28,985
Sound too simple to be worth four sections of build time?

43
00:02:28,985 --> 00:02:30,610
The simplicity is the point.

44
00:02:30,610 --> 00:02:35,313
The weekly report agent is this same shape with a spreadsheet where the inbox is.

45
00:02:35,313 --> 00:02:38,854
The follow up chaser is this shape with a calendar behind it.

46
00:02:38,854 --> 00:02:43,034
Master one honest version of this diagram and the other three agents are

47
00:02:43,034 --> 00:02:47,388
rearrangements, which is exactly why the course can promise them by Friday.

48
00:02:47,388 --> 00:02:50,813
Do not worry about the unfamiliar words on those boxes yet.

49
00:02:50,813 --> 00:02:55,632
Every label you can see gets explained in Section 2, and none of them need touching

50
00:02:55,632 --> 00:02:55,980
today.

51
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:59,463
An imported workflow arrives with its settings already made.

52
00:02:59,463 --> 00:03:04,340
The only two things it cannot arrive with are your accounts, because those are yours

53
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:06,546
to connect, and that is the next step.

54
00:03:07,096 --> 00:03:12,088
Forty one items is not a typo on my screen, and it will not be the number on yours.

55
00:03:12,088 --> 00:03:16,659
That is how many emails were sitting in the test inbox I built this workflow

56
00:03:16,659 --> 00:03:19,185
against, standing in for Priya's real one.

57
00:03:19,185 --> 00:03:23,876
Once you connect your own account, that number becomes however many emails are

58
00:03:23,876 --> 00:03:26,341
actually sitting in your inbox right now.

59
00:03:26,341 --> 00:03:31,273
The number rides on the line between the boxes, and that line matters more than it

60
00:03:31,273 --> 00:03:31,634
looks.

61
00:03:31,634 --> 00:03:36,626
In n8n, data travels along those wires as small tables you can open and read: here,

62
00:03:36,626 --> 00:03:41,317
one row per email, with the sender, the subject and the text in named columns.

63
00:03:41,317 --> 00:03:46,248
When something goes wrong in any workflow you ever build, the fastest diagnosis is

64
00:03:46,248 --> 00:03:51,300
always the same move: click a wire, look at the table, and see what the next box was

65
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:52,202
actually given.

66
00:03:52,202 --> 00:03:57,074
Learn that reflex in week one and half of Section 7's debugging becomes instinct.

67
00:03:57,624 --> 00:04:00,651
I am not going to pretend that part is invisible.

68
00:04:00,651 --> 00:04:05,717
Connecting an email account means typing in a real address and a real password, or

69
00:04:05,717 --> 00:04:08,806
clicking through your provider's own login screen.

70
00:04:08,806 --> 00:04:13,811
It is the only unglamorous step in this entire twenty minutes, and it is also the

71
00:04:13,811 --> 00:04:19,000
last time this course will ask you to trust a screen you cannot see reasoning happen

72
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:19,433
behind.

73
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:21,472
Two reassurances, both checkable.

74
00:04:21,472 --> 00:04:26,661
The login screen belongs to your email provider, not to n8n, and what n8n keeps is a

75
00:04:26,661 --> 00:04:31,542
saved permission you can revoke from your provider's security page at any time.

76
00:04:31,542 --> 00:04:36,546
Because n8n runs on your own machine, that permission never leaves your computer.

77
00:04:36,546 --> 00:04:41,674
For today, a practice inbox or a personal one you are comfortable with is the right

78
00:04:41,674 --> 00:04:42,601
starting point.

79
00:04:42,601 --> 00:04:45,319
Section 8 teaches the tighter scoping later.

80
00:04:45,869 --> 00:04:50,555
This is the part no screenshot does justice, so run it rather than take my word.

81
00:04:50,555 --> 00:04:55,475
The trigger fires, the emails stream in, and the agent box works through them one at

82
00:04:55,475 --> 00:04:55,885
a time.

83
00:04:55,885 --> 00:05:00,805
It takes a few seconds, not an hour, and when it finishes you can open the agent and

84
00:05:00,805 --> 00:05:05,667
read what it wrote about each email in plain English: this one is promotional, this

85
00:05:05,667 --> 00:05:10,528
one is internal chatter, this one is a client asking about a renewal and nobody has

86
00:05:10,528 --> 00:05:11,758
replied for six days.

87
00:05:11,758 --> 00:05:13,574
That last part deserves a beat.

88
00:05:13,574 --> 00:05:15,272
The agent does not just sort.

89
00:05:15,272 --> 00:05:20,017
It gives you its reasons, in sentences, and you can check them against the actual

90
00:05:20,017 --> 00:05:21,247
email with one click.

91
00:05:21,247 --> 00:05:26,108
Every agent in this course is built to be checkable like that, because an assistant

92
00:05:26,108 --> 00:05:30,970
whose reasoning you can read is one you can correct, and one you can correct is one

93
00:05:30,970 --> 00:05:32,434
you can eventually trust.

94
00:05:32,984 --> 00:05:35,671
If your first run fails, you are not behind.

95
00:05:35,671 --> 00:05:37,869
You are having the normal first run.

96
00:05:37,869 --> 00:05:42,816
The habit to build, starting today, is to read the red box instead of fearing it:

97
00:05:42,816 --> 00:05:47,884
n8n tells you exactly which step failed, and the last line of its message says why,

98
00:05:47,884 --> 00:05:48,434
in words.

99
00:05:48,434 --> 00:05:51,609
Click it, read it, fix that one thing, run it again.

100
00:05:51,609 --> 00:05:56,311
Priya's first run failed on the credential step because she had connected the

101
00:05:56,311 --> 00:06:00,403
practice inbox and then run the workflow against the wrong account.

102
00:06:00,403 --> 00:06:03,212
Ninety seconds later she was watching it work.

103
00:06:03,212 --> 00:06:05,960
Nobody in this course debugs by being clever.

104
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:07,303
They debug by reading.

105
00:06:07,853 --> 00:06:12,667
Run the workflow, and within a few seconds it has read every email in the inbox and

106
00:06:12,667 --> 00:06:16,784
flagged exactly one of them as needing Priya's attention today: Ravi's.

107
00:06:16,784 --> 00:06:18,698
Not because it was marked urgent.

108
00:06:18,698 --> 00:06:23,337
Because it read what he actually wrote and understood what an unanswered renewal

109
00:06:23,337 --> 00:06:27,339
question sitting for six days actually means for Brightwell's policy.

110
00:06:27,339 --> 00:06:31,920
Sit with that for a second, because the next lecture is about the question that

111
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:36,791
follows it in every new user's head: if it can read that one, what else can it read?

112
00:06:36,791 --> 00:06:39,981
Because the honest answer is: everything you connected.

113
00:06:39,981 --> 00:06:41,025
The payslip email.

114
00:06:41,025 --> 00:06:42,707
The message from your doctor.

115
00:06:42,707 --> 00:06:44,388
The thread about a colleague.

116
00:06:44,388 --> 00:06:49,144
It read all of it in the same pass, and it needed to, because triage means reading

117
00:06:49,144 --> 00:06:51,057
everything to find the one thing.

118
00:06:51,057 --> 00:06:55,581
Shrinking that trade until it is clearly worth making is what the rest of this

119
00:06:55,581 --> 00:06:56,451
course teaches.

120
00:06:56,451 --> 00:06:58,654
Do not resolve the discomfort tonight.

121
00:06:58,654 --> 00:06:59,118
Keep it.

122
00:06:59,118 --> 00:07:03,758
One thing before the next lecture: run this against an inbox you are comfortable

123
00:07:03,758 --> 00:07:08,455
with, and write down the one email it flags that you had genuinely lost track of.

124
00:07:08,455 --> 00:07:13,152
That is your own version of Ravi's message, and everything from Section 1 onwards

125
00:07:13,152 --> 00:07:16,748
lands differently once the missed email in the story is yours.
